	GDLLMTunables.SUSPEND_FRAME_SETTLE_MS: "How often (in milliseconds) the editor re-checks that a suspend_game stepped frame has landed. Smaller polls confirm frames sooner at the cost of more checks; the whole call is still bounded by the step budget above.",
	GDLLMTunables.SUSPEND_OUTPUT_SETTLE_MS: "How long (in milliseconds) the console is given to catch up before a capture reads it — after a suspend_game stepped frame (a suspended game answers the debugger slowly, and the point of stepping one frame is to see what it printed) and after a reload_game_scripts push, which runs at the game's own pace the same way. Raise it if either capture on a slow machine comes back missing prints.",
	GDLLMTunables.GAME_REPLY_MARGIN_MS: "How much longer (in milliseconds) than the input sequence itself the editor waits for send_game_input's reply before reporting it unanswered. The sequence's own duration is always granted; this is the slack on top for the game to compose its report.",
	GDLLMTunables.GAME_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS: "How long (in milliseconds) the editor waits for a game command's reply — snapshots and method calls (read_game_ui, inspect_game_node, call_game_method) and suspend_game's per-frame confirmation pings — before reporting the command unanswered rather than waiting forever. Raise it for a game whose frames run long: the agent inside the game can only answer between frames.",
	GDLLMTunables.BREAK_ADVANCE_TIMEOUT_MS: "How long (in milliseconds) one debug_game step press may take to land the next break before the result reports that the game ran on instead. Raise it on a heavy game whose single line takes longer to reach; a resumed game that never breaks again is reported as running either way.",
	GDLLMTunables.BREAK_CONTINUE_WATCH_MS: "After debug_game resumes a paused game, how long (in milliseconds) the editor watches for an immediate second break (the next breakpoint or error) before reporting the game as simply running. Shorter than the step timeout on purpose — most resumes just run on.",
	GDLLMTunables.BREAK_EDITOR_FRAMES: "How many editor frames the script editor is given to build the text control whose gutter carries a breakpoint marker before set_breakpoint reports it couldn't. Raise it only if arming or clearing breakpoints fails on a machine where the script editor opens slowly.",
